  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to authorizing certain\nfacilities to provide treatment for the mental health and health care needs of\nindividuals admitted for a substance use disorder",
    "basePrintNo": "S3525",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3525-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 63,
       "fullName": "Timothy M. Kennedy",
       "imgName": "429_timothy_m._kennedy.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 429,
       "sessionMemberId": 2019,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "KENNEDY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §19.17, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S3525\n\nSPONSOR: FERNANDEZ\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to authorizing\ncertain facilities to provide treatment for the mental health and health\ncare needs of individuals admitted for a substance use disorder\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nAuthorizes facilities for the care and treatment of the mentally disa-\nbled to provide treatment for the mental health and health care needs of\nindividuals admitted for a substance use disorder\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the mental hygiene law by authorizing facilities for\nthe care and treatment of mental health and health care needs for indi-\nviduals admitted for a substance use disorder.\n\nSection 2 states the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe NYS Legislature held public hearings exploring the incidence of\nmental health disorders among those seeking/receiving substance use\ndisorder treatment, the barriers to receiving adequate mental health\nservices while in treatment, and the barriers to integrating mental\nhealth services with substance use disorder treatment.\n\nFrom the National Institute on Drug Abuse:\n\n\"Many individuals who develop substance use disorders (SUD) are also\ndiagnosed with mental disorders, and vice versa. Multiple national popu-\nlation surveys have found that about half of those who experience a\nmental illness during their lives will also experience a substance use\ndisorder and vice versa. Although there are fewer studies on comorbidity\namong youth, research suggests that adolescents with substance use\ndisorders also have high rates of co-occurring mental illness; over 60\npercent of adolescents in community-based substance use disorder treat-\nment programs also meet diagnostic criteria for another mental illness.\"\n\nThe chief concerns voiced by advocates in these hearings were that\nmental health services were not integrated with substance use disorder\ntreatment and individuals with dual diagnoses were \"ping-ponged\" between\ndifferent types of facilities or simply refused admittance to a\nsubstance use disorder treatment facility.  Oftentimes, people success-\nfully complete substance abuse disorder treatment but relapse after\ndischarge as their mental health issues come to the fore. Then they\nresume substance abuse treatment without addressing the underlying caus-\nes of addiction. Treating co-occurring mental health disorders at the\nsame time and in the same place where a person is receiving substance\nabuse treatment is designed to address this revolving door problem.\nFinding separate facilities for the treatment of mental health disorders\nis difficult and inefficient. Mental health treatment can and should be\nprovided where the person is already receiving treatment for substance\nabuse.\n\nTo start the process of developing a better plan for treating co-occur-\nring disorders, the state-run addiction treatment centers must be\nauthorized to treat both conditions. There is no reason they cannot do\nso, as psychiatrists, psychologists, and others in private practice\nalready do so. This legislation provides this authorization.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022: S9087 / A10211\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:;\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
